Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 104, "THE BROKEN KINGDOM"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: The object Lyra holds is a lattice token of pale slate, deliberately NOT a stone: the film's grey stone is Ithren's Memory stone, found at Kharesh, and Ormel Hesh has none. Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode. It follows the Season 1 bible. Canon this draft respects: Ormel Hesh is a colony the Aurelith lost on the day of the Sundering and no stone is there; the season shows Auren's involuntary time-stutter only once (episode 101, Continuity Lock 5), so here he has a collapse and a vision and nothing else; Auren and Lyra do not meet (Lock 3), so he reaches her team only as a voice and a handwriting; Torin does not appear (Lock 6). The two-hands set piece is the fort's inner gate. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

EXT. ORMEL HESH — THE WALL OF A HILL-FORT — AFTERNOON (THE DAY OF THE SUNDERING)

A small stone town on a green hill, a day's ride from the white city of the Aurelith, its walls warm in late sun. Washing on lines. Children's voices. A bell marking the quarter hour.

On the rampart a girl of ten, ILSA, sits swinging her legs with a clay cup of something warm in both hands. She is looking east, toward the city, which she can just make out on its cliff.

Above the city, in a daylight sky, seven small lights in a ring.

A low note rises beneath the hill. Ilsa hears it first. She lowers the cup. She says, to nobody:

ILSA
It's humming.

The seven lights flare together. The sky, to the east, tears like cloth, silently, a bleeding seam of light across the blue.

The bell does not finish its stroke. The cup in Ilsa's hand, the tea tipping from its rim, stops. The washing hangs mid-billow. A bird at the top of its wing-beat is held in the air like a pressed flower.

On the wall of Ormel Hesh, the afternoon does not end.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

EXT. ORMEL HESH — THE RUINS — DAY (PRESENT)

The same hill, bare now, the walls broken down to knee height, a green skin of turf over everything. A jeep, three tents, a generator. A hand-lettered sign on a stake: ANVAR / ORMEL HESH SURVEY, PHASE 1.

LYRA THORNE strides up the slope with a roll of plans under her arm, a field jacket, a flask. Behind her, HOLLIS carries a crate; TUAN carries the array and a camera that is, for once, off; and PRIYA RAO, 22, a graduate student in a brand-new hat, carries everything else with the cheerful terror of someone on her first real dig.

PRIYA
Dr Thorne, is it true this is a colony the maps don't have?

LYRA
It's true the maps don't have it.

PRIYA
Is that because it's secret?

HOLLIS
(not unkindly)
It's because it's a bit of grass on a hill, Priya.

Tuan sets the array down at the fort's single surviving gateway, a two-metre arch of fitted stone. He kneels to switch it on. He looks at the display. He says nothing.

LYRA
What?

TUAN
(carefully)
It's running a clean line again.

HOLLIS
The ridge hum?

TUAN
It's here too. Same frequency. Nine point one seven one seven.

The four of them stand at the arch in the still, sunlit grass. A bird crosses the sky, a single dark flick of wing.

LYRA
(decisively)
We go in.

INT. ORMEL HESH — THE INNER KEEP — DAY

A low stone passage, roofless, open to the sky. Beyond it, an inner court, a well, three broken doorways. The light is full, bright, late afternoon.

They arrive in file. Tuan checks his watch: 3:42. Hollis pours tea from a flask into the lid, takes a sip. Priya squats by the well, photographing its collar. Lyra stands in the centre, turning slowly, plans out, looking for the structure that matches.

A bird crosses the sky: a single dark flick of wing.

LYRA (CONT'D)
Hollis, did you see that?

HOLLIS
What?

LYRA
That bird. It's the second time.

HOLLIS
(laughing)
It's a bird, Lyra.

Tuan checks his watch. 3:42.

Hollis lifts the flask lid for a sip and stops with it halfway to his mouth. He looks at the tea. It is hot. He took a sip a moment ago. The level in the lid has not changed.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
(slowly)
How long have we been in here?

TUAN
(not looking at his watch; looking at it)
It's 3:42.

HOLLIS
It was 3:42 when we came in.

A bird crosses the sky: a single dark flick of wing.

Priya has stood up from the well. She is no longer smiling. She is looking at a low doorway on the far side of the court, a dark rectangle in the stone, and she is walking toward it.

LYRA
Priya.

PRIYA
(dreamily; not stopping)
It's lovely in there. It's so quiet.

Hollis moves first. He catches her sleeve at the threshold. And the afternoon resets.

3:42. Tea hot. The bird. They are at the passage, in file, as though they had only now arrived. Priya squats by the well, photographing the collar, happily, as if she had never stood anywhere else. The only difference: she is a little nearer the doorway than before.

LYRA
(very quiet, to Tuan)
Time me.

She watches the bird. She counts. The bird crosses. She counts to a long steady number, and Tuan, eyes on his watch, says without inflection:

TUAN
Eleven minutes.

HOLLIS
Eleven minutes and it starts again.

LYRA
(too calm)
Then we have eleven minutes. Every time. Find out what it wants.

ACT TWO

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — DAY

Hundreds of kilometres from the hill. A bright working morning. Auren, at his bench, tweezers in hand, a coin under the loupe. Nell, at hers, humming along to nothing.

The coin on his tray gives its quiet note. He looks up. Nell has not heard it. He puts down the tweezers, very carefully, as if the bench were glass.

AUREN
(softly)
Nell.

NELL
(not looking up)
Mm.

AUREN
I think I'm going to be unwell.

She looks. His face is the colour of paper. He sways. She is across the room before his hand finds the bench edge, but he is already falling, slowly, as in water.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. ORMEL HESH — THE INNER KEEP — LOOP (AUREN'S VISION)

The keep, in a hush: flat gold light, a low hum. Auren stands at the passage mouth, uninvited, hands at his sides. He is not here, and he knows it, the way a sleeper knows.

Four people stand in the court with their backs to him. A woman, in a field jacket, her hand to her mouth, counting. A man with a flask lid. A man with a headset. A young woman at the well, taking a picture over and over of the same stone.

AUREN
(quietly)
Where am I?

Nobody turns. A bird crosses the sky.

He looks past them to the far wall, where an inner gate stands in the stone, a plain high lintel with two shallow hollows set in either post, a body's width apart, each the shape of a human hand, worn smooth by a very long time.

On the ground before it, the woman in the field jacket kneels with a palm-size slab of pale slate in her hands, scored with nodes and lines. It is a token of the lattice, not one of the seven stones. She is holding it with both hands, hard, as if it might run away.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(without knowing how he knows)
She's holding it.

Each time the bird crosses, he sees the loop tighten: the light a little more yellow, the shadows a little shorter, the woman's knuckles whiter. She is gripping the slate harder every cycle. And every cycle, the loop is shorter.

Auren steps forward. He is shaking. The slate is humming in her hands like a trapped animal.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(to her back, almost pleading)
Don't hold it.

She does not hear. He tries again, nearer, louder, as if the volume were the problem, and then understands that it isn't. He stops. He breathes in. The note under the hill is waiting for him, patient, and he gives it his voice.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(quietly, certainly)
Don't hold it. Hand it across.

CUT TO:

INT. ORMEL HESH — THE INNER KEEP — DAY

The court. Lyra on her knees at the inner gate, white-knuckled around the slate. Tuan, headset over one ear, the array beside him, says:

TUAN
(very still)
Lyra.

LYRA
(not turning)
Not now.

TUAN
Something's on the line.

He pulls the headset off. A thin tone leaks from the cups, and under the tone, shaped by it as a throat shapes air, a voice. A young man's voice. Hardly there.

AUREN (V.O.)
(through the headphones, from nowhere)
Don't hold it. Hand it across.

Hollis lowers the flask. Priya, at the well, turns, blinking, as if she had been woken. Lyra looks at the slate in her hands and at the two hollows in the gate posts, a body's width apart, each worn to the shape of a palm.

LYRA
(slowly)
It's a lock.

HOLLIS
A lock with two hands.

LYRA
(already moving)
Tuan. Here. On the other post.

Tuan crosses to the far post. He puts his right palm in the hollow. Lyra, at the near post, puts her left in the other. The slate is between them, in the air, resting on both their fingers.

LYRA (CONT'D)
(through her teeth)
Don't let go.

TUAN
(white-faced)
Wasn't planning on it.

A silence. The hum swells. The gate does not open. Lyra's grip tightens by reflex.

AUREN (V.O.)
(patient, in the headphones)
Hand it across.

Lyra shuts her eyes. She opens her fingers.

The slate does not fall. It lifts a hair's breadth in the air between their palms, held by nothing, humming, then settles gently into Tuan's hand.

For a long second, nothing. And then, from the lintel, a sound like a breath long held. The gate swings in on a bright, ordinary afternoon.

A bird crosses the sky. The hum stops.

PRIYA
(from the well, blinking, in a small voice)
Did I fall asleep?

HOLLIS
(gruffly, helping her up)
For a very long time.

Tuan looks at his watch. It reads 3:54. For the first time, it moves.

ACT THREE

EXT. A RIDGE ABOVE ORMEL HESH — DUSK

A bare ridge half a mile from the ruins. Vesper Lund lies at its crest in a dark coat, a pair of field glasses at her eyes, a radio at her hip. Beside her, with a flask and a calm, patient expression, CORVIN HALE.

Through the glasses, tiny and distant: four figures coming out of the keep into the dusk, one with a slate in her hand. They are laughing, in the exhausted way people laugh after a close call.

VESPER
(into the radio, low)
It's open. They opened it.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
(over the radio, warm, tired)
The loop?

VESPER
Finished. Nobody was lost. A student walked in and was brought out.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
Ormel Hesh.

A short silence on the line. Vesper lowers the glasses.

MALACHAR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(quietly)
It was the first town to stop. Ilsa. A child. I knew her father.

Vesper says nothing. Corvin, beside her, looks out at the dusk and, for once, does not make a joke.

VESPER
(slowly)
Someone has found the lattice.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
I know.

VESPER
(carefully)
Then what do we do?

MALACHAR (V.O.)
(after a very long breath, almost to himself)
We wait for them to find the next one. And we are kind.

The radio goes quiet. The dusk deepens on the empty ruins.

INT. A TENT AT ORMEL HESH — NIGHT

A lantern, a camp table, a pot of tea. The four of them in blankets, drained, quiet, happy. Priya asleep in a camp chair with her hat over her face. Hollis nodding off over a crossword. Tuan, headphones round his neck, replaying something on his laptop with a frown.

Lyra sits alone at the far end of the table with her field notebook open before her and a pencil in her hand. She is writing up the day, and she is not happy with what she is writing.

LYRA
(under her breath)
"A voice on the line." No. A young man. How could a young man...

She stops. Turns a page back, to a drawing she made at the gate that afternoon, half remembered, half instinct: the slate, the posts, the two hands.

And beside it, on the facing page, she sees something she did not draw.

INSERT — THE NOTEBOOK. A diagram in neat pencil: a ring of seven nodes joined by fine lines, a smaller circle at the centre. Her own handwriting stops at the margin. There, beside the figure, in careful square capitals, as if written by a child grown up, five numerals in a row:

9 1 7 1 7

Lyra does not move. She holds her breath. She does not recognise the hand. She cannot say why it feels like being looked at.

LYRA (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
Who drew that?

Nobody answers. In the sleeping camp, under the canvas, under the hill, a very low note holds.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — INFIRMARY — NIGHT

A narrow cot, a blue blanket, a lamp. Auren, pale, opens his eyes. Nell sits beside him in a plastic chair with a stopwatch in her hand, her hair a mess.

NELL
(very tired)
Eleven minutes.

AUREN
(hoarse)
What?

NELL
You were out for exactly eleven minutes. I timed it. I thought you'd want to know.

He looks at the ceiling. At his hands. He turns them over slowly, as if they might say something.

AUREN
(barely audible)
I said something. Didn't I?

NELL
(carefully)
You said, "Don't hold it. Hand it across." Twice.

A long silence. The lamp hums, very faintly.

AUREN
(to himself)
Hand it across.

Nell takes his hand in both of hers, and he does not pull away.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 104 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
